olicy if  Trump succeeds in hurting Mexico with  these new tariffs that will make the  Mexican economic situation worse  which will create  a demand for more Mexicans to try to  come to the United States legally and  illegally which is what Donald Trump is  trying to stop these tariffs are  completely counterproductive we've gone  from Mexico is definitely going to pay  for the wall - Mexico is not paying for  the wall and now there isn't even a wall  - we are going to put tariffs on  avocados and cars and all of the stuff  that comes from Mexico we're gonna make  that more expensive for Americans while  potentially increasing the motivation  that Mexicans will have to come to the  United States illegally I thought we  were told that businessman president  genius Trump was gonna bring us economic  prosperity we were told that this is  some kind of complex 4d chess that  Donald Trump is executing on trade which  many of his followers during the  campaign who by the way know nothing  about trade people who know nothing  about trade fell hook line and sinker  for the idea that Donald Trump knew how  to fix it it's not happening this is  triggering the possibility of a  recession and that probability keeps  growing a second trump term could be  utterly devastating to the economy of  this type of completely uninformed  tariff policy continues and the most  important and stunning thing about this  is that both Donald Trump and his  followers and some people who aren't his  followers to be quite fair and full  don't understand that when you tariffs  China or Mexico they don't pay for it  the importing companies and people in  the United States are the ones that pay  for it now I I don't want to ignore the  sort of economic theory behind the  tariff the the idea is that even though  strictly speaking the tariff is paid by  the importing countries companies and  individuals if we bring avocados from  Mexico to the US and they have a 5%  tariff it's the u.s. importer that pays  the 5% which they pass along to the  consumer so avocados end up costing 5%  more the economic theory behind it is if  you let's imagine that American avocados  are 3% more expensive than Mexican  avocados if you put a five percent  tariff on the Mexican avocado the idea  would be you will boost local business  because you now have the option of  either Mexican avocados with a five  percent tariff on top or American  avocados which are only three percent  more expensive these are imaginary  numbers so you would shift consumption  from the Mexican avocado to the American  avocado the problem is that we are  starting to tariff items that are not  the the you can't easily shift  production in the way that would be  required for it to work in that way when  we're talking about aluminum and steel  from China which are building materials  that are you don't just switch your  supplier overnight companies don't have  that flexibility they're not nimble in  that way when there is a certain demand  for avocados that's based on the fact  that we are getting them from Mexico you  can't just put a five percent tariff on  avocados and all of a sudden it's all  American avocados that everybody's  buying the supply is not there it takes  a while to grow these things cars it  takes a while if you're buying cars that  are built in Mexico and a tariff gets  placed on them  you can't immediately shift production  in order to account for that in a way  that will work the way Trump maybe  believes it would work so we're gonna  dig into that more after the break but  once again markets are realizing Trump  doesn't know what the hell is going on  with these tariffs let me know what you  think I'm on Twitter at D Pakman the  show is on twitter at david pakman show  today's sponsor is Skillshare and i'm  excited to tell you that everybody in  our audience is getting two entire  months of free access to Skillshare when  you click the link underneath this video  skill shares the site that o